Skywave — live DSC monitor with transmitter position estimates
==============================================================

A browser view onto routine maritime radio traffic. Skywave tunes
public KiwiSDR receivers to the Digital Selective Calling channels
(MF 2187.5 kHz and five HF frequencies), decodes every burst, and
prints who is calling whom as a scrolling log.

For each call it also estimates where the transmitter is. Once one
receiver has decoded a burst, every receiver on that band is searched
for the same waveform and timed against it; the arrival-time
differences give a position and an error ellipse. The estimate is
shown next to the ship's latest AIS position.

How good the estimate is depends on the time of day. In daylight the
signal follows the sea surface and timing is good to tens of
microseconds. At night it also arrives by ionospheric reflection, and
estimates are tens of kilometres off at best. The page marks night
estimates.

Using it
--------

Open https://research.datadesk.eco/skywave/. Pick a region from the
dropdown. Tap a row to see the call, the estimate and the receivers
that heard it. Tap anywhere to enable audio; the page plays the
strongest burst.

Running it
----------

    PORT=8085 node server/skywave.mjs

The client in `client/` is static; its `skywave-gateway` meta tag
names the server. `CLAUDE.md` describes the method, what has been
measured against AIS, and how it is deployed.
